WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:07.420
I don't think many people are familiar with the idea

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.089
that boredom can be stressful.

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:11.630
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:12.140
Yeah.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:15.450
Since so many kids are bored.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:21.320
Yes, and it's certainly profoundly seen on the behavior

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:25.130
when students were asked why they're dropping out of high school.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.890
The number one response was boredom.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:31.090
And when they were asked to break it down-- what do you mean, boredom?

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:33.300
It would be, the subject wasn't interesting.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.410
Or it would be, the information wasn't relevant to me.

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:42.110
So boredom is certainly a problem in terms of what happened, the outcome.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:47.910
But with boredom and repetition, it is very much like the confinement and lack

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:55.460
of choice that stresses animals put in cages or people put in prisons

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.720
or confined against their will.

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:05.730
The brain is a searching, surviving structure that

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:09.050
is out to explore and make sense of the world

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:15.480
and to be able to respond to input, and when there's lack of choice,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.530
that sense of confinement is frightening, it's threatening,

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.950
and it threatens the sense of survival.

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.300
So the brain goes on high alert.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:27.530
The stress goes up.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.620
The lower brain gets to be in charge.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.790
And as I said, the neural networks conducting-- with neuroplasticity,

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:39.200
it gets more and more efficient wiring of the system into the lower brain

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.210
and disconnect from the upper brain.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.270
Boredom reflects a global judgment that you

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.220
don't think there's information to be gleaned from the environment,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.900
so it's better to ramp down the energy you're expending.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:54.870
But what's really interesting is the act of being

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:59.470
bored itself creates stress on the brain because your brain doesn't

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:00.350
want to be bored.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:02.230
It's seeking out information.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.020
And so that stress is going to compel you

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:07.350
to act to try to solve that problem, which in a classroom

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.789
usually looks like disruption or other kinds of things.

00:02:10.788 --> 00:02:13.800
So we have this really terrible place we're

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:18.380
in when you design on average-- you create situations

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.120
where kids that fall behind get stressed from that, which

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:23.950
then impairs their ability to learn.

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:29.270
But the kids that could keep moving are fine, are actually held back,

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:33.110
and they're bored, which creates stress, which has an impairment on learning.

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:36.640
So you've got these environments that are really serving nobody.

